{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 1 exit — RCA: the downgrade is REPRODUCED on `/review/`, not merely unguarded\n\nThe ticket recorded \"no plaintext Location has been OBSERVED from these three surfaces; this is a\nmissing structural guarantee, not a reproduced downgrade.\" **That is now superseded by direct\nevidence.** An unauthenticated probe of the deployed edge reproduces the downgrade on `/review/`.\n\n### Live evidence (unauthenticated, no credential transmitted)\n\n    $ curl -sS -o /dev/null -D - https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/review/health/\n    HTTP/2 307\n    location: http://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/health\n\n    $ curl … /review/webhook/   -> HTTP/2 307  location: http://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/webhook\n    $ curl … /review/rerun/     -> HTTP/2 307  location: http://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/rerun\n\nSame shape as 79b2: a **307** (method AND body preserved) carrying an **absolute `http://`\nsame-host** Location. Clients differ on whether they keep `Authorization` across a same-host\nscheme change, so a client that keeps it retransmits its credential in plaintext — the review-bot\nauthenticates callers with an `X-Rebar-Token` header (`src/rebar/review_bot/app.py:63`), which is\nexactly such a credential.\n\n    $ curl … /opcert/jobs/      -> HTTP/2 403   (no Location)\n    $ curl … /opcert/health/    -> HTTP/2 403   (no Location)\n\n`/opcert/`'s fail-closed `X-Opcert-Guard` check (`infra/nginx/rebar.conf.template:118-123`) rejects\nan unauthenticated probe **before** proxying, so the live probe cannot reach the app. That is a\nreachability answer, not an exemption — see the local reproduction below.\n\n### Confirmed mechanism — this is 79b2's root cause B, on two more services\n\nAnswering the ticket's explicit open question (\"`/review/` and `/opcert/` should be checked for the\nsame posture rather than presumed affected or unaffected\"): **both share the broken posture.\nNeither is exempt.**\n\n`FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS` is set for exactly one service — `mcp` (`infra/compose/docker-compose.yml:357`,\nmirrored in the blue-green path at `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh:380`). It is **unset** for\n`review-bot` (`docker-compose.yml:75`) and for `opcert` (`docker-compose.yml:266`). uvicorn reads it\nnatively, defaults it to `\"127.0.0.1\"`, and wraps the app in\n`ProxyHeadersMiddleware(trusted_hosts=<that>)`. Both services run **inside containers** published on\nhost loopback — `127.0.0.1:${REVIEW_BOT_PORT:-8000}:8000` (`:97`) and `127.0.0.1:8090:8080` (`:298`)\n— so host nginx's connection arrives from the **docker bridge gateway**, not `127.0.0.1`. The peer\nis untrusted, `X-Forwarded-Proto: https` is dropped, `scope[\"scheme\"]` stays `\"http\"`, and\nStarlette's `redirect_slashes` builds its redirect as `URL(scope=…)` — an **absolute** URL whose\nscheme comes from that scope.\n\nDiscriminating experiment (local, both real apps, one variable changed — the trust list):\n\n    review-bot /health/       trusted='127.0.0.1'  -> 307 http://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/health\n    review-bot /health/       trusted='*'          -> 307 https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/health\n    opcert     /opcert/jobs/  trusted='127.0.0.1'  -> 307 http://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/opcert/jobs\n    opcert     /opcert/jobs/  trusted='*'          -> 307 https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/opcert/jobs\n\nThe `127.0.0.1` row reproduces the live `/review/` header byte for byte, and flipping only the trust\nlist flips only the scheme. That both confirms the mechanism and rejects the rival hypotheses\n(nginx stripping the header; an app-side hardcoded scheme; an HSTS gap — HSTS is present and\nserver-level at `rebar.conf.template:98`). It also establishes that **opcert emits the identical\ndowngrade** to the one caller that can actually reach it: the guard-bearing API Gateway request,\ni.e. the caller that carries a credential.\n\n### Gerrit `location /` — CAN emit, but does NOT downgrade\n\nGerrit does emit absolute same-host Locations, and they are already `https`:\n\n    /settings       -> 302  location: https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/settings/\n    /Documentation  -> 302  location: https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/Documentation/index.html\n    /login          -> 302  location: https://github.com/login/oauth/authorize?...&redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2F...\n\nBecause Gerrit builds them from `canonicalWebUrl = https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/`\n(`infra/compose/gerrit.config:14`) rather than from a per-request forwarded scheme. So Gerrit is\n**not** affected by root cause B — but its guarantee rests on one config value that nothing at the\nedge enforces. It is the one surface where the ticket's original \"missing structural guarantee,\nnot a reproduced downgrade\" framing still holds exactly.\n\n### Discovered, deliberately NOT fixed here\n\n`/review/`'s Location also **loses the `/review/` prefix** (`…/review/health/` -> `…/health`, which\nfalls through to Gerrit and 404s). That is a separate path-correctness defect caused by the\nprefix-stripping `proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:${REVIEW_BOT_PORT}/;` combined with an app that\nbuilds absolute URLs from the public Host header. It is orthogonal to the scheme invariant this\nticket owns, it is not a security downgrade, and folding it in would widen the blast radius past\nwhat this plan covers. Filed as a sibling with `discovered_from`.\n\n### Fix shape (both layers, exactly as 79b2 did for MCP)\n\n1. **Root cause.** Set `FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS: \"*\"` on `review-bot` and `opcert`, so each app trusts\n   nginx's forwarded proto and stops building `http://` URLs at the source. Bounded exactly as for\n   `mcp`: both ports are published on host **loopback** with nginx the sole reachable client, and\n   nginx sets `X-Forwarded-Proto https` as a **literal** that overwrites anything a client sends,\n   so the value cannot be forged.\n2. **Structural backstop.** Add per-location `proxy_redirect` http->https rules to `/review/`,\n   `/opcert/` and Gerrit's `location /`, so no future upstream — or a Gerrit whose\n   `canonicalWebUrl` drifts — can re-open the downgrade. **Per-location, never server-level**\n   (79b2's binding precedent), and each block restates `proxy_redirect default;` first so the\n   implicit default that nginx drops as soon as any explicit rule appears is preserved."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787953829451319001,"uuid":"f39caadd-c3a7-4322-90ac-1e8bd46f4445"}